// bash line continuation and here-documents as a source PRE-PASS.
//
// bash removes an unquoted backslash-newline while it READS its input, before any token
// exists, so it may split a name (`x\<nl>y=3` assigns xy), a keyword, an operator or a
// ${...} anywhere. Kept as written: inside '...' and $'...', in a comment, and in the body of
// a here-document whose delimiter is quoted. In an unquoted body the joined line is what is
// compared with the delimiter (`E\<nl>OF` ends it). Inside "${...}" a single quote, $'
// included, is an ordinary character.
//
// A here-document's body starts on the line after the COMMAND line, wherever on it the
// marker stands (`cat <<A; echo x`, `f <<A <<B`). BsSplice moves each body next to its
// marker, as `<<A\x02body\x03`, so HdScan reads marker and body as one token, and drops it
// from where it stood.
//
// The rewrite goes through c.setSrcMap() and restarts the parse; the map it hands over says
// where each run of the new text came from, so c.lineOf and a parse error still name the
// line and column as written. A file with neither a backslash-newline nor a "<<" leaves
// after two indexOf.
// Include with :include("lib/bash-splice.abnf") and reference BsSplice first in the start
// production.
